376 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 830 0 R
/E (In this Arduino program, a touch sensor is connected to pin 2 \(TOUCH_PIN\), and two LEDs are connected to pins 6 \(LED_LOW\) and 7 \(LED_HIGH\). Each LED indicates a different state of the sensor.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [29]
>>
endobj
377 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 831 0 R
/E (The setup\(\) function runs once when the Arduino starts. It sets the touch sensor as INPUT and both LEDs as OUTPUT using pinMode\(\). Initially, both LEDs are turned OFF.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [30]
>>
endobj
378 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 832 0 R
/E (The loop\(\) function runs continuously. It reads the touch sensor value using digitalRead\(\) and stores it in touchState.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [31]
>>
endobj
379 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 833 0 R
/E (If the sensor is touched \(HIGH\), the LED on pin 7 turns ON and the LED on pin 6 turns OFF. If there is no touch \(LOW\), the LED on pin 6 turns ON and the LED on pin 7 turns OFF.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [32]
>>
endobj
380 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 93 0 R
/E (So overall, one LED indicates touch detection while the other shows when there is no touch.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [33]
>>
endobj
